  "kind": "new finite construction",
  "attribution_note": "Classical hexagonal honeycomb with complete guillotine composition. PingYou Ltd supplies the finite translational seam returns and complete native certificates; the source lattices are not claimed as new.",
  "construction": {
   "name": "whole_hexagonal_column_seam_return",
   "proof": "Heterogeneous whole-patch rows and layers\n\nThis extends HONEYCOMB_LAYER_PROOF.txt by composing different complete honeycomb patches rather than requiring a single patch shape in every layer. Each module has actual ab whole hexagonal-prism owners, with the same source lattice word and full patch boundary costs as before. A fixed proper signed axis permutation puts its bounding box dimensions into (width,row-height,layer-height).\n\nFor one exact layer-height h, a row type consists of floor(S/width) identical complete modules at that fixed frame, centered across a common width S. Its cost is its full row-height, and its value is ab times the module count. A row word with summed heights at most S gives an actual layer with that many whole owners: place the closed row intervals consecutively, center the combined rows, and retain their different module widths. Distinct rows have disjoint box interiors.\n\nA layer word with summed full layer-heights at most S then gives a complete cube packing, by placing those layers on consecutive closed intervals and centering the stack. Different layers have disjoint box interiors. Every owner in a module has the inherited honeycomb orientation, further acted on by the module's fixed proper cube symmetry. The grammar's max-plus count is therefore constructive: its full backpointer word is an inverse decoder, not only an upper or density estimate. Deleting a named whole-owner suffix preserves a complete packing.\n\nAll costs lie in (p+q*sqrt(3))/2 with nonnegative integers p,q. The finite dynamic program stores (p,q) costs and an actual word, first for rows at one layer height, then for layers. The metric comparison only orders those exact positive costs against the common cube side; no angle or independent physical scale is optimized. A source module that has no count advantage may still occur in an equal-value boundary word and is retained in the candidate bank. The final native witness verifies all actual vertices and pair separation independently of the grammar.\n\nThe hexagonal whole-column seam return\n\nUse the canonical hexagon with vertices(+-1,0),(+-1/2,+-sqrt(3)/2). Keep vertical column period v=sqrt(3), and let neighboring columns alternate between offsets0 anddelta for0<=delta<=sqrt(3)/2. Set horizontal step h=2-delta/sqrt(3). The physical diagonal face-contact equation is(sqrt(3)/2)*h+delta/2=sqrt(3), equal to twice the hexagon's support in that unit direction. Neighboring columns therefore remain disjoint for every permitted delta, while same-column owners separate in y and columns two or more apart separate in x since2h>=3>2.\n\nFor c columns and r rows, retain every whole owner at(h*i,v*j+delta*(i mod2),0). The complete patch has dimensions(2+(c-1)*h,r*v+epsilon*delta,L), epsilon=1 ifc>1 and0 otherwise, with midpoint((c-1)*h/2,(r-1)*v/2+epsilon*delta/2,0). At delta=sqrt(3)/2 this recovers the honeycomb patch; atdelta=0 it recovers the rectangular column boundary. Every intermediate point is a physical return with the same owners and fixed canonical frame, not an angle variation.\n\nFix a full heterogeneous guillotine word from the rigid-patch control bank. Give each row one common delta. Enforce module_count*row_width<=S, sum(row_heights)<=S in each layer, one common layer thickness at least each row's module depth, and sum(layer_thicknesses)<=S. These explicit linear inequalities retain the complete patch end costs and give a physical inverse decoder after solving. Retain the original rigid word, chosen row deltas, layer thicknesses, and every whole-owner deletion. Independent native wall and pair checks certify the serialized construction.\n",
